    "final_answer": "I've confirmed through multiple sources:\n1. Luke Bhan's personal website (lukebhan.com) shows his email is lbhan@ucsd.edu and he's located in San Diego, CA\n2. His LinkedIn profile indicates he's a \"Graduate Student at UC San Diego\"\n3. His previous papers (like IEEE transactions) confirmed his affiliation\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n- **Most recent paper related to \"graph neural networks\" on ArXiv:** arXiv:2609.30173 - \"GridSFM: A Foundation Model for Solving AC Optimal Power Flow\" (submitted 24 September, 2026)\n- **First author:** Luke Bhan\n- **Affiliation of the first author:** **University of California San Diego (UCSD)**\n\nThe paper combines a physics-inspired graph neural network to predict optimal solutions to AC-OPF problems. The first author, Luke Bhan, is a graduate student at UC San Diego (confirmed by his email lbhan@ucsd.edu on his personal website and his LinkedIn profile).</mm:think>Based on my research, here is the answer to the task:\n\n## Summary\n\n**Most recent paper related to \"graph neural networks\" on ArXiv:**\n- **arXiv ID:** 2609.30173\n- **Title:** \"GridSFM: A Foundation Model for Solving AC Optimal Power Flow\"\n- **Authors:** Luke Bhan, Weiwei Yang, Margaret Capetz, Baosen Zhang\n- **Submitted:** 24 September, 2026\n\nThe paper proposes a foundation model for predicting the optimal solution of an alternating current optimal power flow (AC-OPF) problem using a physics-inspired **graph neural network**.\n\n## First Author's Affiliation\n\n**Luke Bhan** — **University of California San Diego (UCSD)**\n\nThis was confirmed through multiple sources:\n1. His personal website (lukebhan.com) lists his email as lbhan@ucsd.edu and his location as San Diego, CA\n2. His LinkedIn profile identifies him as a \"Graduate Student at UC San Diego\" researching the intersection of scientific machine learning and control theory\n3. He holds B.S. and M.S. degrees from Vanderbilt University (previous affiliation), and is currently pursuing his PhD at UC San Diego",
